WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.342
Rust helps prevent data races in safe
code,

2
00:00:02.422 --> 00:00:05.646
but defensive programming still matters in
concurrent and async systems.

3
00:00:05.726 --> 00:00:08.899
Shared state, locks, channels, atomics,
task lifetimes, cancellation,

4
00:00:08.979 --> 00:00:11.231
timeouts, connection pools, file handles,
queues,

5
00:00:11.311 --> 00:00:13.870
and memory limits can all affect security
and reliability.

6
00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:15.485
Memory safety is a major advantage,

7
00:00:15.565 --> 00:00:17.664
but it is not the same as operational
resilience.

8
00:00:17.744 --> 00:00:20.200
Shared state needs deliberate
synchronization design.

9
00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.504
Traits such as Send and Sync express
important thread-safety properties,

10
00:00:23.584 --> 00:00:25.836
but developers still choose the
concurrency model.

11
00:00:25.916 --> 00:00:29.498
A lock may protect memory safety while
still creating deadlock or starvation risk.

12
00:00:29.578 --> 00:00:33.263
A channel may simplify ownership while
still allowing unbounded work to accumulate.

13
00:00:33.343 --> 00:00:36.311
Async Rust adds additional design
questions around task ownership,

14
00:00:36.391 --> 00:00:39.001
cancellation, timeout behavior, and
blocking operations.

15
00:00:39.081 --> 00:00:42.049
A service can fail because it accepts
unbounded work, waits forever,

16
00:00:42.129 --> 00:00:43.306
blocks the wrong executor,

17
00:00:43.386 --> 00:00:45.843
or holds a lock across an operation that
should not block.

18
00:00:45.923 --> 00:00:49.096
These are defensive design issues, not
just performance tuning details.

19
00:00:49.176 --> 00:00:51.018
Resource controls are security controls.

20
00:00:51.098 --> 00:00:54.169
Bounded queues, connection limits, request
timeouts, worker limits,

21
00:00:54.249 --> 00:00:58.036
backpressure, and graceful degradation
help preserve availability when the system is

22
00:00:58.116 --> 00:00:58.576
stressed.

23
00:00:58.656 --> 00:01:02.290
A memory-safe application can still become
unavailable if it does not control work,

24
00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.264
memory, and external dependency pressure.

25
00:01:04.344 --> 00:01:07.005
Defensive Rust designs concurrency around
clear ownership,

26
00:01:07.085 --> 00:01:10.053
bounded resources, observable failure, and
recoverable behavior.

27
00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:13.613
Reviewers should ask what happens when
requests spike, dependencies slow down,

28
00:01:13.693 --> 00:01:16.098
tasks are cancelled, or partial work must
be cleaned up.

29
00:01:16.178 --> 00:01:19.300
The safest concurrent design is the one
future maintainers can explain.
